E.7  THE PREFERRED FRAME (L31).  The dipolar medium has a 4-velocity u^mu (its rest frame) and a dipole 4-vector xi^mu.
     The medium's rest frame is a distinguished timelike direction, and xi^mu is dynamical (it propagates), so u is realised
     by MATTER and propagates -- L61's arm (1c), a preferred frame carried by a propagating medium, as for the superfluid.
  [PASS] E7  GATE 3 CARRIED: the medium supplies a preferred frame (its rest frame u^mu), realised by matter and propagating (the dipole field is dynamical) -- L31's projector realised by a medium, L61 arm (1c); not a non-propagating foliation   (u^mu = the medium's 4-velocity; the dipole xi^mu propagates, so the frame propagates -- same status as the superfluid's condensate)

======================================================================================================================
PART G -- THE INTERNAL SECTOR: mode count and HEALTH of the force that sources the dipoles
======================================================================================================================
The brief's known-hard sector: the medium must STAY polarised and stable.  Two things are computed -- the mode count of
the dipole sector, and whether the polarised, weakly-clustered state is a stable configuration or an unstable equilibrium.

G.1  Mode count.  Beyond GR's 2 tensor modes, the medium adds a pressureless fluid (the monopole sigma: 0 new PROPAGATING
     gravitational modes, it is dust) and a dynamical dipole 4-vector xi^mu.  The dipole's spatial part xi^i (3 components)
     carries the polarisation; with the internal potential W(Pi) its longitudinal mode is the MOND-active one.  Total field
     content: 2 (tensor) + 3 (dipole vector) = up to 5 sectors, of which the longitudinal dipole is load-bearing.
      mode count: 2 tensor + a matter fluid (0 propagating grav modes) + a dynamical spatial vector xi^i (<= 3) -> the internal sector
      is a vector field with a potential, health decided by its kinetic sign and by the coupled polarisation+gravity response, next.

G.2  The load-bearing instability.  MOND requires the medium to sit near PERFECT ANTI-SCREENING: the polarisation nearly
     cancels the field, y = 4 pi G Pi ~ g, so the residual g_b is small.  Define the medium's effective gravitational
     permittivity for its OWN density perturbations, epsilon = dg_b/dg = 1 - 4 pi G/W''(Pi).  As the system goes deeper into
     MOND, epsilon -> 0, so the effective Newton constant for the monopole, G_eff = G/epsilon, DIVERGES.
      epsilon(g) = (2 g/a0)/(1 + 2 g/a0):  at g = 0.01/0.1/1.0 a0  epsilon = 0.020, 0.167, 0.667  -> G_eff/G = 1/epsilon = 51.0, 6.0, 1.5
      the smooth monopole's collapse time is shorter than standard free-fall by sqrt(epsilon) = 0.41 at g = 0.1 a0: the MOND-tuned
      medium is MORE Jeans-unstable exactly where it must stay smooth.  'Weak clustering' is not a stable state but a fine-tuned unstable one.
  [PASS] G1  the internal sector is UNSTABLE in the MOND regime: reproducing MOND forces the effective permittivity epsilon -> 0 (near perfect anti-screening), so the monopole's effective Newton constant G_eff = G/epsilon diverges and the smooth state the 'spent once' result needs (D1) is a fine-tuned unstable equilibrium -- a polarisation requiring an unstable configuration is not a theory   (epsilon(0.01 a0) = 0.020, G_eff/G = 51; collapse faster than free-fall by sqrt(eps) = 0.41)

     THE PINCER, stated as the theorem it is:  dipolar dark matter escapes L61 by making the phantom real gravitating mass
     (so it PASSES lensing, E1) -- but the SAME construction that makes the polarisation nearly cancel the field (perfect
     anti-screening, needed for MOND) makes the monopole's self-gravity diverge, so the smooth monopole that 'spent once'
     REQUIRES (D1) cannot be maintained.  If the monopole stays smooth, the state is unstable; if it clusters, L61 fires
     (D1: overshoot 1.692).  The lensing pass is real and structural; the death is in the internal sector, not at lensing.

